What is DNS?
DNS (Domain Name System) is like the directory service of the internet.

- Every computer/server has an IP address (like 142.250.182.78).
- But we humans prefer to remember names (like google.com).
- DNS translates the name into the correct IP address so your browser knows where to connect.

⚙️ Why is DNS important?
- IPs of servers can change (when systems restart, autoscaling happens, or servers are replaced).
- If applications use hardcoded IPs, you’d need to manually update them each time the IP changes.
- With DNS, you only map the name to the new IP once (e.g., in Route 53).
Applications just keep using the domain name — no need to touch the services.

📞 Real-life Example — Phone Numbers
Think about calling a friend:
- You don’t memorize their phone number every time.
- You just save their name in contacts.
- When you click their name, your phone automatically finds the correct number and connects the call.

👉 DNS works the same way:
- You type google.com.
- DNS looks up the IP address (like looking up the phone number).
- Your system then connects to the right server.
- Even if your friend changes their phone number, you just update it in contacts once. From then on, calling them still works.
 That’s exactly how DNS saves us from updating IPs everywhere.
